# The State of the Kazhagam?
Tamil title: கழக நிலை?

For nearly two months, the Kazhagam has remained without movement or activity.

Periyar's marriage was a spear hurled at the party's honour. The movement's dignity will be levelled to the ground by its leader's improper act. Even if one "raises one's voice and circles the entire town," one cannot repair the loss of honour caused by the leader's course. His self-interest has carried him to the point of casting into the dust the ideals we praised and propagated. Countless branches, comrades, and members of the Executive Committee have withdrawn in tears, saying: "There is no longer any benefit in serving under him. Every drop of sweat we shed through our labour will merely become water that we pour upon his 'private' field. His leadership is therefore unacceptable, and until it changes, we shall stand apart from the Kazhagam's work."

With the party's nerves and flesh no longer moving, every one of its activities stands "paralysed."

"I alone am the leader, the writer, the speaker..."

Periyar has hitherto claimed that the movement grew through him alone. The volunteers who lost legs, eyes, and property and acquired the scars of sacrifice never received the esteem that he did. He went on saying that "the party's growth is due to me," but his heart never acknowledged the answer to the question, "Through whom did it truly grow?"

Not only did the leader fail to praise the Kazhagam's volunteers, he also kept them dancing to his threats. What is service? What benefit does the service of an individual volunteer bring to the party? Is not the movement's growth the collective result of its volunteers' service? Those engaged in Kazhagam work had never before reflected upon these questions.

Because their attention was fixed upon singing the leader's praises, individuals did not even pause to consider the quality of their own service.

Today, however, this truth has become as plain as a mountain in broad daylight. It is now clear where the labouring strength associated with the Kazhagam resides. It has become evident that the volunteers' service is of supreme importance to its growth. Events have made everyone understand that the volunteers are all the nerves, blood, and flesh of a party.

After seeing party work remain "at a standstill" for two months, it is natural that comrades who laboured day and night for the party's growth should ask, "Can things remain like this?" It is natural for such a thought to arise among those concerned with its development. This thought is indeed worthy of praise.

Kazhagam work has not taken place for two months. This "motionless condition" has demonstrated the greatness of the volunteers' service. It is now known that the volunteers are the party's vital artery and that the movement moves only when they move.

Because the immense power of selfless warriors for principle has withdrawn from Kazhagam work, none of the activities that once occurred now take place; neither party service nor propaganda is being conducted.

This motionless condition should have startled the leader. Had he been free of self-interest, he should have been alarmed and asked, "How have Kazhagam activities that swept forward like a storm come to lie so still?" Had he not regarded office as the greater thing, he should have been distressed.

After realising that this immense force did not want him, had cast aside his leadership, and had lost its trust in him, the leader should voluntarily have stepped down from the chair called the "leadership of the Kazhagam." That is the proper and correct course. Had he done so, he would even have received the praise of honourable people.

Did that happen? The leader considers only his own throne important and does not appear to have thought about self-respect.

Even after understanding that the party itself does not want him, he remains occupied with offering "explanations" and fixes his desire and ambition upon the "glory of leadership." He gives no thought to the party's growth or the continuation of its activities.

"The leader should have resigned of his own accord."

"Do not heed the explanations; let us remove him."

"Let the majority of us who hold fast to the Kazhagam's ideals devote ourselves to carrying out our work."

These are the views expressed in letters written to us by comrades concerned about the Kazhagam's growth.

We have been carefully considering each person's opinion. The eagerness of comrades who say that the Kazhagam's growth must not be obstructed and that the morning star must rise finds an echo in our own hearts. Had the leader considered the party's development important, he would honourably have stepped aside. But his "ideal" does not appear to concern the movement or its growth. Only the contentious attitude, "Well, let us see!" is visible in his conduct. We therefore do not expect him to resign of his own accord.

Consequently, measures must now be taken to build the Kazhagam. No more time should be allowed to pass. A sound decision must be reached, and our work for the country must blossom again as it did before.

Decisions to this effect must be made within another week or two. We trust that the necessary work will begin. No opportunity will be allowed for the Kazhagam's present condition to continue. The morning star will soon rise. Pain and sobbing will cease, and we shall walk upon the path to victory.

*Dravida Nadu*, 21 August 1949.

---NOTES---
`Kazhagam` is retained as the historically specific organization at the centre of the 1949 leadership dispute. The article was published after Periyar's marriage and shortly before the formation of the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam. Its repeated bodily metaphor—volunteers as the party's nerves, blood, flesh, and vital artery—is preserved.
